The consolidated artifact is complete. Let me provide the response.


From Flow 0: concrete per-file LoC numbers in §2 (foundation, chat, layout
buckets); the three-layer Foundation/Domain/Product mental model; pre-filled
verdicts in Phase C based on evidence; the AF widget name-collision risk; the
theme-tokens-required column flag; quality gates and downstream smoke test in
§7.

From Flow 1: explicit R1–R8 deterministic rule table with sub-labels
(re-sync/new/parameterize/decouple-hook/decouple-api/domain-coupled/compositio
n); the "re-sync vs new" distinction critical for the AF-pedigree files; the
hidden raw-fetch coupling risk and B3 grep pattern that catches both helpers
and raw fetch; explicit ~106-file folder enumeration; snapshot metadata
requirement; per-rule rationale citation (Rx) as a hard validation check (C4).

Restructured content: §0 evidence summary → §1 approach with R1–R8 rules → §2
evidence inventory → §3 catalog schema (13 columns + 9-section layout) → §4
execution phases A–G with refactor recipes broken out → §5 unified 14-risk
register (4🔴/6🟡/4🟢) → §6 files-to-create/modify → §7 validation with C1–C4
+ 10 sub-checks → §8 self-validation.

Quality of integration: The two upstream plans had significant overlap (same
evidence base, similar three-bucket classification, near-identical risk
shape), but each had non-trivial content the other lacked. Consolidation added
genuine value by merging the rules framework (Flow 1) with the layered model
and pre-filled verdicts (Flow 0), unifying the 13-column schema, and
harmonising the risk register from 8+9 distinct items into 14 deduplicated
risks. The Phase C pre-fills now reference the rule IDs from Flow 1, which
neither input did. That said, the marginal information gain over either single
artifact is moderate rather than transformative — a single competent
implementing agent could have produced either input and arrived at a correct
catalog.

