  canonical_contract/00-widget-protocol.md (~½ day)\n 219 \n 220 Goal: lock     
  the React ↔ Python contract.\n 221 \n 222 Document, with snippets and a JSON  
  envelope example:\n 223 \n 224 1. Wire format. Match WidgetMessage.to_dict()  
  byte-for-byte:\n 225    json\n 226    { "widget_id": "...", "widget_type":    
  "single_choice",\n 227      "title": "...", "description": "...",\n 228       
  "input_mode": { "mode": "...", "prompt": "...", "options": [...] },\n 229     
  "fields": [ { "name": "...", "widget_type": "...", ... } ],\n 230             
  "metadata": { ... } }\n 231    \n 232 2. Canonical string-id table (from      
  widget_protocol.py): text_input, single_choice, multiple_choice, dropdown,    
  toggle, tool_argument_form, confirmation (to be added to Python), plus RE     
  extensions proposal_selection, grouped, multi_input. Aliases (free_text →     
  text_input, multiple_choices → multiple_choice, press_to_continue →           
  confirmation) flagged as deprecated with target removal version.\n 233 3.     
  JSON Schema for WidgetMessage and WidgetResponse (inline). This eliminates    
  the three-fallback-shape problem (config.input_mode.options vs                
  config.options vs config.choices).\n 234 4. Response envelope.                
  WidgetResponse.to_dict() shape: {widget_id, values, action ∈                  
  {submit|cancel|skip}}. React onSubmit(result) MUST emit this exact shape      
  (replacing ad-hoc {choice_index}, {custom_text}, {values} payloads).\n 235    
  5. React <WidgetHost> contract. Wrapper component that, given a               
  WidgetMessage JSON, calls registry.get(widget_type) and renders it; on        
  submit invokes onResponse(WidgetResponse). Each widget React component MUST   
