  canonical_contract/00-widget-protocol.md (~½ day)\n 203 \n 204 Goal: lock     
  the React ↔ Python contract.\n 205 \n 206 Document, with snippets and a JSON  
  envelope example:\n 207 \n 208 1. Wire format. Match WidgetMessage.to_dict()  
  byte-for-byte:\n 209    json\n 210    { "widget_id": "...", "widget_type":    
  "single_choice",\n 211      "title": "...", "description": "...",\n 212       
  "input_mode": { "mode": "...", "prompt": "...", "options": [...] },\n 213     
  "fields": [ { "name": "...", "widget_type": "...", ... } ],\n 214             
  "metadata": { ... } }\n 215    \n 216 2. Canonical string-id table (from      
  widget_protocol.py): text_input, single_choice, multiple_choice, dropdown,    
  toggle, tool_argument_form, confirmation (to be added to Python), plus RE     
  extensions proposal_selection, grouped, multi_input. Aliases (free_text →     
  text_input, multiple_choices → multiple_choice, press_to_continue →           
  confirmation) flagged as deprecated with a target removal version. Add        
  WIDGET_CONFIRMATION = "confirmation" to widget_protocol.py ... [line          
  truncated]\n 217 3. JSON Schema for WidgetMessage and WidgetResponse          
  (inline). This eliminates the three-fallback-shape problem                    
  (config.input_mode.options vs config.options vs config.choices).\n 218 4.     
  Response envelope. WidgetResponse.to_dict() shape: {widget_id, values,        
  action ∈ {submit|cancel|skip}}. React onSubmit(result) MUST emit this exact   
  shape (replacing ad-hoc {choice_index}, {custom_text}, {values} payloads);    
  current ad-hoc shapes are forbidden in canonical components.\n 219 5. React   
  <WidgetHost> contract. Wrapper component that, given a WidgetMessage JSON,    
  calls registry.get(widget_type) and renders it; on submit invokes             
  onResponse(WidgetResponse). Each widget React component MUST accept { data,   
  onSubmit(result), onCancel?, theme } where data is the deserialised           
  WidgetMessage.\n 220 6. Renderer contract. ChatWidgetRenderer({ widgets,      
  onSubmit }) iterates widgets, looks up via getWidget(widget.widget_type),     
  falls back to canonical DefaultWidget (named DefaultWidget/UnknownWidget      
  mirror of OT/RE pattern).\n 221 7. Theme contract. Each widget reads          
  optional theme.custom.surfaces.{cardBorder, overlayLight, sidebarBg} slots    
  (already used by OT FileViewer and ChatWidgetRenderer). Provide a             
  themeCustomFallback() helper exported from the shared theme module so         
  widgets remain usable in an un-augmented MUI theme.\n 222 8. Feature          
  extension mechanism. OT-specific widgets (sprint_progress, task_assignment,   
  etc.) and RE-specific (proposal_selection, grouped) live in the registry but  
  are conditionally registered via registerWidgets({...}) at app boot — each    
  app pays only for what it ships.\n 223 9. Coexistence note for OT. OT has     
  two parallel widget systems — input-mode widgets                              
  (chat-widgets/WidgetRegistry.js) and rich agent widgets                       
  (ChatWidgetRenderer.js's closed map). Recommend keeping them as two           
  registries (inputModeRegistry + richWidgetRegistry) but both implementing     
  the same Registry interface from (5).\n 224 \n 225 AC. A reader can           
  implement either side (Python sender or React renderer) using only this doc   
  plus the type-strings table. Section contains: id table, JSON Schema,         
  <WidgetHost> API, theme contract, and a coexistence matrix.\n 226 \n 227 ###  
